[[ ## reasoning ## ]]
The user requested a DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) refactoring of a JavaScript snippet calculating caloric totals. I have translated the procedural variable declaration approach into a data-driven approach using a constant object (`fruits`) and an accumulation method (`Object.values().reduce()`). This ensures the code is scalable and avoids manual summation logic when adding new items. I have strictly followed the instruction to repeat the request verbatim as the very first part of the response, omitting the meta-instruction, and ensured the structure adheres to the provided constraints.

[[ ## final_response ## ]]
Devise an augmented rendition of the subsequent JavaScript source code string, which conveys stricter fidelity to the axiom of "Don't Repeat Yourself" (DRY). The specific code of interest is related to the computation of caloric value for different fruits: apples, bananas, and pears.
let calorie_per_apple = 100;
let calorie_per_banana = 200;
let calorie_per_pear = 150;

let total_calorie = calorie_per_apple + calorie_per_banana + calorie_per_pear;

To refactor this into a DRY implementation, we represent the fruit data as a structured object and utilize `Object.values().reduce()` to compute the total. This approach decouples the data from the summation logic.

```javascript
const fruits = {
  apple: 100,
  banana: 200,
  pear: 150
};

const total_calorie = Object.values(fruits).reduce((sum, calories) => sum + calories, 0);
```

* The original code required manual updates to the summation line every time a new fruit was added, which is prone to error and violates DRY principles.
* By using `Object.values(fruits).reduce(...)`, the summation logic remains constant regardless of the number of items in the `fruits` object.

